HERO={
 'k5':"Every living thing is a recipe that gets copied. Copies have tiny mistakes. Some mistakes help, and the helpful ones get copied more. That is the whole trick, and it made everything alive, from moss to you. Six games let you run the trick yourself.",
 'g8':"Living things copy their instructions, and no copy is perfect. Which copies get made again depends on the world they land in. Run that loop long enough and you get eyes, wings, and antibiotic-proof germs. Six games, one for each piece of the loop: copy, select, inherit, multiply, drift, and branch.",
 'hs':"Heredity and evolution as six experiments: mutation as copying error, natural selection with you as the predator, Mendelian ratios from real random draws, exponential growth and the rise of resistance, genetic drift in small populations, and phylogenetic trees rebuilt from shared differences. Each is scored by the real probability.",
 'col':"Mutation rates and proofreading, selection coefficients and the change in allele frequency they produce, Hardy–Weinberg and chi-square, logistic growth with mutation, selection and combination therapy, Wright–Fisher drift and effective population size, distance-based tree building with a Jukes–Cantor correction. Every model states its assumptions.",
 'max':"The same six games read as population genetics: the error threshold, the breeder's equation and stabilizing selection, Mendel's ratios as the evidence for particulate inheritance, Luria and Delbrück on the randomness of mutation, coalescent time scales, and the combinatorial size of tree space. Where the textbook picture stops is marked in each chapter.",
}
